  "query_preview": "/Saturday through Wednesday/...\nIssued 319 AM MDT WED OCT 7 2026\nHigh temperatures on Saturday will be 15 to 25 degrees cooler \nthan Friday behind the cold front. Winds will be breezy over the\nweekend, expanding to lower elevations as the front stalls \nsouth of the area. The upper level trough will dive south \nthrough Washington and Oregon over the weekend, potentially \ndeveloping into a closed low and bringing a chance of light rain\nand high mountain snow to the area. The highest chances \n(30-60%) will be across Idaho where the low will tap into higher\nmoisture. Even cooler air will arrive on Sunday as the low \nmoves south, dropping highs to 5 to 10 degrees below normal. \nSnow levels will vary across the area, lowest across southeast \nOregon closer to the low center at 4500 to 6000 feet MSL, and \n5500 to 7500 feet across southwest Idaho.\n\nThe low is expected to continue south into California late \nSunday into early next week and interact with tropical moisture\nfrom the remnants of Hurricane Rachel. The bulk of the moisture\nis expected to pass to the east of our area late Sunday, but \nmay clip southern Idaho with potential for significant rain \namounts. The low will become cut off from the parent trough \nmoving east across Canada, resulting in ensemble differences on\nthe track of the low as it slowly moves east. A further south \ntrajectory would keep deeper moisture out of our area, but \nfurther north could result in significant precipitation amounts \nearly next week. Temperatures will remain below normal, but \ngradually moderate to near normal by Wednesday. Additionally, \nwith cool air in place, frost will be possible in lower valleys \nespecially if a drier pattern develops. Winds will subside early\nnext week.",
